                    "text": "Your setup is actually in good shape for this — the key idea is that the `@Tool` method is the security boundary, and that's what makes it *safer* than handing the model HTTP access directly.\n\n## The mental model\n\nThe LLM never sees your API key and never makes the HTTP call. It only sees the JSON schema Spring AI generates from `BookTools.java:19` (method name, `@Tool` description, parameter types). So:\n\n- **Never** give the model a \"call this URL\" tool or let it pass through headers/auth from the prompt.\n- **Always** keep the credential in the Java process, and expose narrow, typed parameters (an author name, an ISBN, a listing id) — not a URL, not a raw JSON body.\n\nThat mirrors what you already do with `bookRepository` in `BookTools.java:13`: the model asks for an operation, the server decides how to execute it.\n\n## What to keep server-side\n\n| Concern | Where it lives |\n|---|---|\n| Base URL | Config property, validated at startup — not a tool param |\n| API key | Env var / secrets manager, injected into the client |\n| Which endpoints are reachable | One tool method per allowed operation |\n| Timeouts, retries | On the HTTP client |\n| What the model sees | Trimmed DTO mapped from the response |\n\n## Concrete pattern\n\n**1. Config** — replace the deepseek placeholder style with env-var placeholders so nothing lands in git:\n\n```properties:src/main/resources/application.properties\nexternal.api.base-url=https://api.example.com\nexternal.api.key=${EXTERNAL_API_KEY}\nexternal.api.connect-timeout=3s\nexternal.api.read-timeout=5s\n```\n\n`spring.datasource.password=` and `spring.ai.deepseek.api-key=` at `application.properties:6,9` are blank in the repo — good — but `application-local.properties` holds a real key in plaintext. Same rule applies to the new one: keep it out of the repo and rotate the DeepSeek key if it's ever been shared.\n\n**2. One `RestClient` bean, locked down** — webmvc already gives you `RestClient`