d\n                .toList();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nPoints worth calling out:\n\n- `.uri(uri -> ...)` with `.build(title)` URL-encodes the value — this is what stops injection/SSRF attempts from a model-authored argument. Don't build URLs with string concatenation.\n- The tool calls one fixed path. There is no path segment the model controls.\n- Returning a small DTO keeps tokens down and prevents leaking internal fields the external API included.\n\n**4. Register it** — `ChatController.java:31` currently does `.tools(bookTools)`. Add the new bean as a constructor param and pass both:\n\n```java\n.tools(bookTools, bookSearchTools)\n```\n\n**5. Fail closed in the tool** — Spring AI catches exceptions from `@Tool` methods and feeds the error text back to the model, so don't let raw upstream bodies (which can contain tokens or stack traces) escape:\n\n```java\n.onStatus(HttpStatusCode::isError, (req, res) -> {\n    throw new IllegalStateException(\"External catalog unavailable\");\n})\n```\n\nLog the real detail server-side with SLF4J; the model only gets the generic message.\n\n## Two things to flag in your current code\n\n- There's no SLF4J logging anywhere — every tool uses `System.out.println` (`BookTools.java:21`). When you add an external dependency, you'll want a real logger to distinguish \"upstream 401\" from \"upstream 503\", and `System.out` won't give you that.\n- No timeout config exists anywhere today. For an external API this is the difference between a degraded tool call and a hung request thread, so set them explicitly on the client rather than relying on defaults.\n\nIf you want, I can implement this against a specific API — tell me which one and I'll wire the client, config, and tool method in."
